From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm6375: add the soundwire TX wake interrupt

Jack detection on this port only works because a udev rule pins the WCD9370
TX soundwire slave permanently awake:

	ATTR{power/control}="on"

MBHC raises nothing while that slave is suspended, so without the rule
plugging headphones in does nothing at all. The cost is that the slave, and
with it its master, never enters runtime suspend -- the port burns power to
keep a jack detector listening.

That should not be necessary, and the pieces are nearly all there already.
wcd937x-sdw.c declares the TX device able to wake the bus:

	pdev->prop.wake_capable = true;
	pdev->prop.simple_clk_stop_capable = true;

and the Qualcomm master implements the wake path -- but it takes the wake
line from the *second* interrupt of the controller node:

	ctrl->wake_irq = of_irq_get(dev->of_node, 1);
	if (ctrl->wake_irq > 0)
		devm_request_threaded_irq(..., qcom_swrm_wake_irq_handler, ...);

sm6375.dtsi gives swr0 one interrupt, so wake_irq is never set and the
slave has no way to signal while the link is stopped. The binding has had
the second one all along -- qcom,soundwire.yaml documents
interrupt-names = "core", "wakeup" -- and sm8450, sm8550, sm8650 and
sc8280xp all declare it. sm6375 simply never did.

The line is a TLMM GPIO on this SoC rather than another GIC SPI, which is
why it needs interrupts-extended. The number is the vendor's, from the
rhodep audio overlay, against the same controller (swrm-io-base 0xA740000,
which is swr0, the one clocked by txmacro):

	interrupts-extended =
		<&wakegic 5 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
		<&tlmm 128 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
	interrupt-names = "swr_master_irq", "swr_wake_irq";

GPIO 128 is unclaimed in this port. The trigger is given as LEVEL_HIGH to
match the driver, which requests it with IRQF_TRIGGER_HIGH regardless of
what the device tree says.

This only adds the wake path. The udev rule stays for now: removing it is
the point of the exercise, but it should not be removed until the codec has
been shown to detect a jack from runtime suspend on real hardware.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
@@ -1914,7 +1914,9 @@
 		swr0: soundwire@a740000 {
 			compatible = "qcom,soundwire-v1.6.0";
 			reg = <0x0 0x0a740000 0x0 0x2000>;
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 296 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupts-extended = <&intc GIC_SPI 296 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+					      <&tlmm 128 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupt-names = "core", "wakeup";
 
 			clocks = <&txmacro>;
 			clock-names = "iface";
